-- title:
--   Integers prime to r are units in a π-adically complete domain
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ be a prime number and let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring which is a domain, and let $\pi \in \mathcal O$. Assume: $\mathcal O$ is adically complete with respect to the ideal $(\pi) =$ `Ideal.span {π}` (in Mathlib's sense, comprising Hausdorffness and completeness for the $(\pi)$-adic filtration); the quotient $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has cardinality exactly $r$ (as a `Nat.card`, so in particular it is finite of that order); and the ideal generated by the image of the natural number $r$ in $\mathcal O$ coincides with $(\pi)$. Then for every natural number $m$ not divisible by $r$, the image of $m$ under the canonical map $\mathbb N \to \mathcal O$ is a unit of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that in a complete local ring with residue field $\mathbb F_r$ an integer prime to the residue characteristic is invertible. It supplies the invertibility hypotheses `IsUnit (m : 𝒪)` needed to count points and to form moduli data over the abstract $\pi$-adic base $\mathcal O$ in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, and is cited by a large number of statements there.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_isUnit_natCast_of_not_dvd_of_card_quotient.lean

